At one point in the past, we were talking about collecting stormwater runoff. I can't remember if it was at the

00:36:05.890 --> 00:36:06.530
community

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:08.770
center or somebody else's

00:36:09.410 --> 00:36:10.770
and the importance of doing that.

00:36:11.825 --> 00:36:15.105
And while we had a lot of rain in 2023,

00:36:17.905 --> 00:36:20.385
when we have the big rainstorms like we do,

00:36:21.025 --> 00:36:24.545
a lot of the rain just runs off. It doesn't have the chance to soak into the ground.

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:27.200
And so that leads

00:36:27.440 --> 00:36:28.160
to

00:36:28.960 --> 00:36:30.640
a lack of groundwater.

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:34.560
The other thing when we get the big storms

00:36:35.280 --> 00:36:36.080
is that,

00:36:37.385 --> 00:36:39.705
we saw a lot of flooding with

00:36:40.265 --> 00:36:40.905
twice.

00:36:42.025 --> 00:36:46.905
I can't remember if the first big flood was in the end of December or early January,

00:36:46.905 --> 00:36:47.305
but

00:36:48.025 --> 00:36:50.980
twice within the last couple of weeks, we've had some significant

00:36:51.060 --> 00:36:54.020
flooding going on around the Hop River area.

00:36:54.500 --> 00:36:56.260
So I went back to take a look,

00:36:57.380 --> 00:37:01.380
and this probably isn't as scientific as it could be because

00:37:02.500 --> 00:37:05.380
going forward, I'll be more meticulous about

00:37:06.465 --> 00:37:10.785
does 0.5 go into this category or this category here?

00:37:12.545 --> 00:37:14.305
And it didn't make a lot of difference.

00:37:14.785 --> 00:37:16.385
What I was surprised at

00:37:17.185 --> 00:37:18.625
was we had

00:37:18.945 --> 00:37:20.545
about as many

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:24.070
rain events of greater than an inch

00:37:24.870 --> 00:37:27.270
as we did less than an inch.

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:31.110
And in fact, we had 13 rain events over the last year

00:37:31.430 --> 00:37:33.110
that were greater than two inches.

00:37:33.875 --> 00:37:38.275
And when we were looking at stormwater runoff with the development

00:37:38.275 --> 00:37:39.715
of the lake regs in that,

00:37:40.195 --> 00:37:41.155
we thought

00:37:41.635 --> 00:37:43.155
a two inch rain

00:37:43.315 --> 00:37:43.955
storm

00:37:44.195 --> 00:37:46.035
was an unusual occurrence.

00:37:46.035 --> 00:37:46.915
And certainly

00:37:47.330 --> 00:37:48.690
last year wasn't.

00:37:48.850 --> 00:37:51.730
I went back and looked at the data from 2022

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:52.450
and

00:37:54.530 --> 00:37:55.090
was

00:37:55.490 --> 00:37:57.970
'22 was much more as you would expect,

00:37:58.290 --> 00:37:59.970
but we're starting out 2024

00:37:59.970 --> 00:38:03.755
with some pretty good rate events as well. So anyhow,

00:38:04.075 --> 00:38:04.795
I

00:38:04.955 --> 00:38:10.155
was doing my engineer thing, going through the data, thought I'd share that with all you guys so that

00:38:10.555 --> 00:38:11.355
you can

00:38:12.155 --> 00:38:15.035
see where we are and how

00:38:16.270 --> 00:38:18.030
the changing rain

00:38:19.070 --> 00:38:20.350
in Andover,

00:38:20.910 --> 00:38:25.310
we might want to do something about that as we develop our plan of conservation and development,

00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:26.990
whether from a flood prone

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:30.190
collecting runoff or whatever. And so

00:38:30.795 --> 00:38:34.635
that's all I had on that, unless there's any questions on that one.

00:38:39.275 --> 00:38:40.635
>> Okay. Everything

00:38:40.635 --> 00:38:43.595
up to two inches is, you know, fairly reasonably close.

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:46.000
What was that, Scott?

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:49.840
>> Everything up to two inches is, you know, fairly reasonably

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:53.200
close. Then when you get to the two inch, a big rainfall,

00:38:53.200 --> 00:38:54.320
it's way up.

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:55.280
>> Yep.

00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:57.440
The other thing I went in,

00:38:57.760 --> 00:39:02.125
you know, we we talk about the hundred year storm, one hundred year flood,

00:39:02.845 --> 00:39:07.245
and I didn't know how much rain that was. And to the best of what I can track down,

00:39:07.805 --> 00:39:11.325
you know, it's a little bit more than six inches in one rainstorm.

00:39:11.405 --> 00:39:16.510
So I went back and looked at last year and we didn't have anything that exceeded

00:39:16.590 --> 00:39:31.195
the one hundred year flood level, know, so it's not happening every year, but we did get close a couple of times. We've had a storm, you know, a couple storms of four inches and five inches. So that's

00:39:31.195 --> 00:39:32.155
getting up there.

00:39:33.675 --> 00:39:34.955
>> I know that we had,

00:39:35.195 --> 00:39:37.035
like, 12 within two weeks

00:39:38.715 --> 00:39:39.915
at one point.

00:39:41.090 --> 00:39:42.450
Yep. So

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:44.290
my ring in

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:46.210
>> Ken.

00:39:46.450 --> 00:39:47.170
Yes.

00:39:47.330 --> 00:39:49.170
It's John Gyszkowski.

00:39:49.250 --> 00:40:00.705
Yep. I just wanted to to me to spin this forward a little bit. Think I appreciate the I appreciate the the research and breakdown, and and that is extraordinary about, like, 13 independent two inch rain events or more.

00:40:01.345 --> 00:40:06.225
I guess my question for the POCD, and this is maybe something we could talk about another time,

00:40:06.625 --> 00:40:08.940
but what are the implications

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:10.060
of that from

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:12.940
a public work standpoint,

00:40:13.180 --> 00:40:17.260
from a municipal impact standpoint? Like, and maybe this is a question for

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:18.940
Eric,

00:40:19.260 --> 00:40:23.925
is this, you know, with the increased rain we saw last year, has

00:40:24.085 --> 00:40:25.925
the town seen

00:40:27.525 --> 00:40:31.525
repeated damage to properties, repeated inundation of certain roads,

00:40:32.805 --> 00:40:40.560
you know, loss of municipal facility? Like, is it is it, you know, is it just a bunch of rain or has it had a real world

00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:52.465
impact in Andover that we need to change our thinking about? I think is is sort of what what's the implication here. It's had an impact across all the towns. Oh, sure. A lot of cleanup afterwards, a lot of putting

00:40:53.025 --> 00:40:55.265
>> stone back in the sides of roads,

00:40:56.305 --> 00:40:57.425
and it's everywhere.

00:40:59.105 --> 00:41:04.130
>> Been terrible. But so, I mean, so the question is, you know, based on that and with the

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:08.450
not crazy assumption that this trend will continue or even worsen,

00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:13.330
is there something fundamental that the town should be doing different

00:41:13.650 --> 00:41:14.930
about either

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:19.135
its maintenance or the way that it builds things new or locates things?

00:41:22.495 --> 00:41:25.455
>> So I think from a public works perspective,

00:41:26.335 --> 00:41:27.295
it's

00:41:27.295 --> 00:41:32.970
something we're quite aware of. I mean, we definitely have a couple of roads that

00:41:33.290 --> 00:41:34.250
flood

00:41:37.930 --> 00:41:39.530
frequently enough

00:41:39.530 --> 00:41:40.890
to be of concern.

00:41:45.015 --> 00:41:47.095
You know, and I think we have

00:41:47.575 --> 00:41:49.015
kind of changed

00:41:49.015 --> 00:41:51.575
what we put on the sides of roads

00:41:53.015 --> 00:41:55.255
to stabilize and handle

00:41:55.735 --> 00:41:56.935
more severe

00:41:57.415 --> 00:41:58.375
rain events.

00:42:01.480 --> 00:42:03.000
But yeah, definitely

00:42:03.640 --> 00:42:08.360
an issue. And I mean, Jay and I have talked about it and one

00:42:09.400 --> 00:42:11.720
of the easy things is just

00:42:12.040 --> 00:42:13.800
pretty much every time

00:42:14.135 --> 00:42:20.855
you know, we change out a culvert, the intention now is to use a bigger sized culvert than we did before,

00:42:21.895 --> 00:42:28.535
you know, and just simply increase the capacity. We do know that there's a couple of roads where existing culvert system

00:42:29.530 --> 00:42:32.010
cannot handle the heavy rain flows.

00:42:32.330 --> 00:42:35.210
We have one bad spot on Gilead Road,

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:37.930
and that is simply

00:42:38.250 --> 00:42:39.610
it is largely

00:42:39.610 --> 00:42:49.125
a question of the capacity of the culvert versus the amount of water that inundates it and it floods out the road. So there are a few of those.

00:42:52.085 --> 00:42:56.325
You know, and the other thing that's been interesting is that as we

00:42:56.890 --> 00:42:59.050
have gotten a little bit warmer,

00:42:59.290 --> 00:43:03.930
we're having more storms that are on the ice continuum

00:43:04.010 --> 00:43:06.410
and less on the snow continuum,

00:43:06.890 --> 00:43:07.850
which actually

00:43:09.370 --> 00:43:11.530
tends to increase our salt

00:43:12.405 --> 00:43:14.325
decrease it. So even though

00:43:14.805 --> 00:43:16.885
it's a little warmer, we're still,

00:43:17.125 --> 00:43:19.765
you know, we've, the last few years,

00:43:19.925 --> 00:43:23.765
we've been using as much or more salt than when it was colder

00:43:23.765 --> 00:43:27.525
and you had more storms that were snowy and less in that intermediate

00:43:27.525 --> 00:43:28.005
stage.

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:31.410
So those are really the two effects

00:43:31.410 --> 00:43:32.450
I've seen.

00:43:34.530 --> 00:43:40.530
There's one change that was pushed down from the Army Corps of Engineers

00:43:40.530 --> 00:43:41.410
and that is

00:43:41.815 --> 00:43:43.335
when you design

00:43:43.335 --> 00:43:45.335
for a bridge,

00:43:45.895 --> 00:43:49.495
the span between the banks used to be 1.2

00:43:49.495 --> 00:43:51.895
times the average bank width,

00:43:52.295 --> 00:43:54.215
bank full width is what they called it.

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:57.740
And now they're looking for 1.5

00:43:57.740 --> 00:44:00.060
times the average bank full width,

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:03.260
which means considerably longer spans.

00:44:04.060 --> 00:44:06.540
And that has a major cost implication

00:44:06.540 --> 00:44:07.340
to the town.

00:44:09.025 --> 00:44:12.065
So I think that's something that's

00:44:12.065 --> 00:44:13.105
a concern.

00:44:15.985 --> 00:44:17.505
You know, and I think from

00:44:17.905 --> 00:44:19.505
a monetary perspective,

00:44:19.505 --> 00:44:20.865
the town really

00:44:21.105 --> 00:44:24.610
has quite a few, you know, very old culverts

00:44:24.610 --> 00:44:27.810
and the culverts were put in during the days when,

00:44:28.530 --> 00:44:32.210
you know, Percy Cook would look at a river and say, ah,

00:44:32.530 --> 00:44:44.975
you know, put in a 72 inches pipe and be done with it. You know, and now there's a lot more to it to to actually measure and and measure the water basin that flows into it and whatnot.

00:44:48.440 --> 00:44:53.560
>> Okay, that's, yeah, thank you. I guess the other thing from, you know, the planning and zoning

00:44:53.720 --> 00:44:54.840
perspective,

00:44:55.880 --> 00:44:56.600
the one

00:44:56.920 --> 00:44:57.720
regulation

00:44:57.720 --> 00:44:58.840
change that

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:00.280
I haven't

00:45:00.845 --> 00:45:07.405
completed yet that we were looking to do is, you know, I think it's Chapter 10, the flood prone zone.

00:45:08.125 --> 00:45:11.565
And we wanted to make it more difficult to build a house

00:45:12.525 --> 00:45:13.485
in that area.

00:45:14.600 --> 00:45:17.480
Based on what we're seeing with the flooding in that,

00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:18.520
I think

00:45:19.000 --> 00:45:19.800
that

00:45:20.920 --> 00:45:23.240
is certainly an important thing to

00:45:24.040 --> 00:45:28.280
discourage building in the flood zone because we seem to be flooding it out

00:45:28.745 --> 00:45:29.545
more

00:45:30.345 --> 00:45:31.785
frequently these days.

00:45:32.345 --> 00:45:35.225
So that's next on my list after I

00:45:35.465 --> 00:45:36.185
finish

00:45:37.705 --> 00:45:40.265
getting done with the batch that we've just gone through.

